---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { author, authorBio, authorBackground, authorNotLawyer, researchMethod, authorLd } from '../data/author';
---
<Base
  title="About PrenupAnswers — Who Runs It & How It's Funded"
  description="PrenupAnswers is an independent educational site about prenuptial agreements for US couples, written by Francisco Gomes Alves from Brazil. Who he is, how we check the facts, and how the site makes money."
  jsonLd={[authorLd]}
>
  <section class="article-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / About</p>
      <h1>About PrenupAnswers</h1>
      <p class="meta">Independent. Educational. Not a law firm — and never will be.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose" id="author">
    <h2>What this site is, and is not</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>PrenupAnswers is a consumer guide.</strong> It exists to help you understand what a prenuptial agreement
      costs, what the process involves, and what questions to take to a lawyer — before you spend money. It is not
      legal advice, and no one here is a lawyer.
    </p>
    <ul class="not-list">
      {
        authorNotLawyer.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <div class="author-profile">
      {
        author.photo ? (
          <img class="author-photo" src={author.photo} alt={author.name} width="140" height="140" />
        ) : (
          <div class="author-photo author-photo--placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
            {author.initials}
          </div>
        )
      }
      <div>
        <h3 class="author-name-lg">{author.name}</h3>
        <p class="author-role-lg">{author.role} · Brazil</p>
        <p>{authorBio}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <a href={author.url}>Full author profile &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p><strong>Background.</strong></p>
    <ul>
      {authorBackground.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Why a Brazilian writes about U.S. prenups</h2>
    <p>
      Because he cannot sell you anything legal. There is no U.S. client to take, no contract to draft, no bill to
      send, and no U.S. address. That removes the two things that usually distort a site like this — the need to win a
      case, and the incentive to keep you paying.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also sets a hard limit on what this site should be trusted to know. We are an outside look at a system we do
      not practise in, which is why every legal claim here is traced to a statute or a published source, and why we
      would rather say "this depends on your state, ask" than guess.
    </p>
    <p>
      His pastoral work is relevant for a narrower reason, and it is the honest one. Couples rarely get stuck on
      doctrine; they get stuck on the conversation — how to raise the subject, what it means that the other person
      flinched, whether refusing means something is wrong. A lawyer can tell you what is enforceable. Only someone who
      has sat with couples can describe what actually happens in the room. This site keeps those two things clearly
      separate and says which is which.
    </p>

    <h2>How we check the facts</h2>
    <p>
      Trust is earned by method, not by biography. This is how the information on this site is put together:
    </p>
    {
      researchMethod.map((m) => (
        <>
          <h3>{m.title}</h3>
          <p>{m.detail}</p>
        </>
      ))
    }

    <h2>How we're funded</h2>
    <p>
      Two ways, and that's it: affiliate partnerships and display advertising. If you click a link to an online prenup
      service and buy something, we may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. See the full
      <a href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Commission never decides what we recommend.</strong> If a cheaper path is the right path for your
      situation, that's what the article says — even when it earns us nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And neither does faith.</strong> The author is a pastor, but this site carries no religious
      recommendations and no religious framing of the decision. It is funded and written as a consumer guide. What
      you believe about marriage is your business; what a prenup costs is arithmetic.
    </p>

    <h2>When you actually need a lawyer</h2>
    <p>
      Online services are fine for straightforward situations. But if one partner owns a business, there's a
      family trust, complex stock comp, real estate across states, or a prior marriage with children involved — talk
      to a licensed family-law attorney in your state. A few hundred dollars of advice up front is cheap insurance for
      a document that governs your finances for life. That advice is worth more than anything on this site, and we will
      say so on any page that would have you thinking otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, corrections, or a source we missed? Email <strong>hello@prenupanswers.com</strong>. Corrections to
      something factually wrong get fixed — if you report one and it is still wrong in a few weeks, email again.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
